comments.message:
Looks like there was a bus name re-used across tests

Looking into `list_names_acquired`
it does `_intf->sd_bus_list_names(_bus.get(), &names, nullptr);`

which calls

```
  _public_ int sd_bus_list_names(sd_bus *bus, char ***ret_acquired, char ***ret_activatable) {
          _cleanup_(sd_bus_message_unrefp) sd_bus_message *reply = NULL;
          _cleanup_strv_free_ char **x = NULL, **y = NULL;
          int r;

          assert_return(bus, -EINVAL);
          assert_return(bus = bus_resolve(bus), -ENOPKG);
          assert_return(ret_acquired || ret_activatable, -EINVAL);
          assert_return(!bus_origin_changed(bus), -ECHILD);

          if (!bus->bus_client)
                  return -EINVAL;

          if (!BUS_IS_OPEN(bus->state))
                  return -ENOTCONN;

          if (ret_acquired) {
                  r = sd_bus_call_method(
                                  bus, 
                                  "org.freedesktop.DBus",
                                  "/org/freedesktop/DBus",
                                  "org.freedesktop.DBus",
                                  "ListNames",
                                  NULL,
                                  &reply,
                                  NULL);
```

which calls all the names acquired on that bus.

If we now do that call in userspace, for the system bus

```
[alexander@alexanderarchlinux phosphor-bmc-code-mgmt]$ busctl call --system org.freedesktop.DBus /org/freedesktop/DBus org.freedesktop.DBus ListNames
as 12 "org.freedesktop.DBus" ":1.0" ":1.1" ":1.2" ":1.3" ":1.4" ":1.12" ":1.22" "org.freedesktop.login1" "org.freedesktop.network1" "org.freedesktop.resolve1" "org.freedesktop.systemd1"
```

we see there are multiple bus names belonging to different processes.

So `list_names_acquired` is the wrong API to get the bus name for the connection.

We would have to go by unique names https://manpages.debian.org/testing/libsystemd-dev/sd_bus_get_unique_name.3.en.html

or keep track of our bus name ourselves and not query it again later on.

Since the common code API does not use unique bus names, i can add a method to `SoftwareManager` class to get our bus name from the unique suffix which is stored in the class.

So we do not have to rebuild that in the test code and avoid any duplication there.


We could also go with `org.freedesktop.DBus.GetNameOwner` to map any name found back to the unique name and then compare with our unique name but that would involve bunch of extra code which is not needed.

```
createInventoryAssociation(true, exampleEndpoint);

createInventoryAssociation(false, exampleEndpoint);
```

I added the assertion for `running` / `activating`, sorry that was somehow missing.
